1
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:37,554
Got it.

2
00:01:44,137 --> 00:01:46,834
This was the last
stop on our tour.

3
00:01:47,007 --> 00:01:49,602
She was so happy to be home.

4
00:01:51,011 --> 00:01:53,071
How did this happen?

5
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:58,941
- How long were you her manager?
- For as long as she had a career.

6
00:01:59,119 --> 00:02:00,143
I discovered her.

7
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:02,585
- When was that?
- Four years ago.

8
00:02:02,756 --> 00:02:05,726
She was singing
in a bar downtown.

9
00:02:06,860 --> 00:02:08,021
It was amateur night.

10
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:09,720
<i>Oh, oh</i>

11
00:02:09,896 --> 00:02:11,941
<i>I gotta tell you, from
the first time I saw her,</i>

12
00:02:11,965 --> 00:02:14,127
<i>I knew she was no amateur.</i>

13
00:02:18,171 --> 00:02:21,266
<i>Tell what I'm thinking of</i>

14
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:25,378
<i>It's a perfect tonic for my love</i>

15
00:02:25,545 --> 00:02:30,745
<i>I think I'm gonna
Spend the night alone</i>

16
00:02:32,352 --> 00:02:34,753
Let's hear it for Kathy.

17
00:02:34,921 --> 00:02:37,220
And Steve and the boys.

18
00:02:39,259 --> 00:02:42,661
We got another performer
coming up, Mr. Barry Bay.

19
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:45,329
- Kathy, you were incredible.
- Thank you.

20
00:02:45,499 --> 00:02:46,933
No. Excuse me. I really mean it.

21
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,831
You're too good to be playing
joints like this. For what, 50 bucks?

22
00:02:50,003 --> 00:02:52,973
- Well, yeah, if I win, heh.
- Oh, you're gonna win.

23
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:54,438
I guarantee you, kiddo.

24
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:55,906
I'm Steve. Fisher.

25
00:02:56,076 --> 00:02:58,136
- Are you one of the judges?
- No.

26
00:02:58,311 --> 00:03:00,940
I'm just somebody who not
only knows talent when he sees it,

27
00:03:01,114 --> 00:03:03,549
but knows what to do with it.

28
00:03:03,717 --> 00:03:05,913
You got anything against
becoming rich and famous?

29
00:03:06,086 --> 00:03:07,611
- Uh-uh.
- Well, that's good.

30
00:03:07,788 --> 00:03:09,051
Here. I'm an artists' manager.

31
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:11,418
I gotta run now.
But you call me.

32
00:03:11,591 --> 00:03:12,854
You got a real future.

33
00:03:14,027 --> 00:03:16,861
<i>Her name was Kathy
back then. Kathy Westfall.</i>

34
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:19,261
<i>And within six months, she
was playing clubs and fairs</i>

35
00:03:19,433 --> 00:03:21,129
<i>under the name of K. C. West.</i>

36
00:03:21,301 --> 00:03:23,395
In less than a year, she
recorded her first album.

37
00:03:23,570 --> 00:03:25,869
Two years later, she
went out on her first tour.

38
00:03:26,039 --> 00:03:28,804
Last year, her third
album went platinum.

39
00:03:29,609 --> 00:03:30,633
Tsk.

40
00:03:32,012 --> 00:03:33,571
Now she's dead.

41
00:03:33,747 --> 00:03:36,114
- Were you at the concert tonight?
- Yeah. Of course.

42
00:03:36,283 --> 00:03:37,876
I attended all her performances.

43
00:03:38,051 --> 00:03:42,546
Was anything, uh,
unusual about this one?

44
00:03:43,857 --> 00:03:44,916
No.

45
00:03:45,092 --> 00:03:48,790
Well, I had a guest
with me, a lawyer.

46
00:03:48,962 --> 00:03:51,932
I was trying to get him to represent
a client of mine in a civil matter.

47
00:03:52,099 --> 00:03:54,534
I figured, if I gave him a
little taste of show business,

48
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:56,431
maybe he would take the case.

49
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:01,704
<i>And though I think
of nothing else</i>

50
00:04:01,875 --> 00:04:05,403
<i>I know someday I'll be okay</i>

51
00:04:05,579 --> 00:04:09,209
<i>It's better this way</i>

52
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:19,251
<i>Yeah, it's better this
way Better this way</i>

53
00:04:34,407 --> 00:04:37,104
Kiddo, you were a sensation.

54
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:39,405
Who are you trying
to snow tonight?

55
00:04:40,147 --> 00:04:44,585
K.C., meet Ben Matlock, a
friend of mine, great fan of yours.

56
00:04:44,751 --> 00:04:46,413
I really enjoyed
your performance.

57
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:48,688
Thank you.

58
00:04:50,957 --> 00:04:52,892
We got to talk. STEVE: Okay.

59
00:04:53,059 --> 00:04:56,325
Why don't you meet Ben and I after
the show for a drink? We'll talk then.

60
00:04:56,496 --> 00:04:57,828
Goodbye, Ben.

61
00:04:57,998 --> 00:05:01,196
<i>K. C., K. C., K. C.!</i>

62
00:05:08,408 --> 00:05:09,919
She's been on tour for
the last three months.

63
00:05:09,943 --> 00:05:11,172
- She's really tired.
- Hmm.

64
00:05:11,344 --> 00:05:13,745
We'll do drinks another time.

65
00:05:13,914 --> 00:05:15,712
Oh, sure.

66
00:05:15,949 --> 00:05:17,850
Sorry, Ben.

67
00:05:18,552 --> 00:05:20,997
<i>He left right after that, not
saying one way or the other</i>

68
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:23,013
<i>whether he'd represent
Jimmy Ray's case.</i>

69
00:05:23,190 --> 00:05:25,625
<i>Naturally, I was a
little ticked off with K. C.</i>

70
00:05:25,792 --> 00:05:27,727
<i>I guess you heard about
what happened next.</i>

71
00:05:27,894 --> 00:05:29,726
You're firing me?

72
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:33,731
For God's sake, Steve, I've
been with you for four years.

73
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,335
My marriage didn't
even last half that long.

74
00:05:36,503 --> 00:05:39,302
- What more do you want?
- Give me a reason. Why?

75
00:05:39,472 --> 00:05:43,739
Steve, you've done all you
can for me. It's time to move on.

76
00:05:44,411 --> 00:05:46,573
- Who put you up to this?
- Nobody.

77
00:05:46,746 --> 00:05:49,716
I'm capable of making
my own decisions.

78
00:05:51,084 --> 00:05:52,416
That is a laugh.

79
00:05:54,654 --> 00:05:57,818
- She hit you?
- Yeah.

80
00:05:57,991 --> 00:05:59,857
Did you hit her back?

81
00:06:00,527 --> 00:06:02,860
No. Of course not.

82
00:06:04,264 --> 00:06:06,233
What'd you do?

83
00:06:06,833 --> 00:06:08,961
Well, I threatened to sue
her for breach of contract.

84
00:06:09,135 --> 00:06:12,128
And then she pointed out the
buyout clause in our original contract.

85
00:06:12,305 --> 00:06:14,900
Said she was gonna exercise
it and went outside to her trailer.

86
00:06:15,075 --> 00:06:16,805
You follow her?

87
00:06:17,244 --> 00:06:18,268
No.

88
00:06:18,445 --> 00:06:22,712
- Were you in her trailer at all tonight?
- No. I wasn't in her trailer all day.

89
00:06:22,883 --> 00:06:24,613
What you got there
on your sleeve there?

90
00:06:25,752 --> 00:06:27,243
I don't know. Stain.

91
00:06:27,420 --> 00:06:29,286
Probably ink from a pen.

92
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:32,789
Okay, Mr. Fisher. I
appreciate your time.

93
00:06:33,193 --> 00:06:34,217
You finished with me?

94
00:06:34,394 --> 00:06:36,386
Unless there's something
else you wanna tell me.

95
00:06:36,563 --> 00:06:37,656
Nothing I can think of.

96
00:06:37,831 --> 00:06:39,823
Okay, well, if we need
you, we'll give you a call.

97
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:41,662
Okay.

98
00:06:45,372 --> 00:06:47,432
Yeah, no, there's still
one more trap case.

99
00:06:47,607 --> 00:06:49,576
Got it. MAN 1:
Yeah, one trap case.

100
00:06:49,743 --> 00:06:51,268
Got it.

101
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:55,010
Stow it away in the corner.

102
00:06:55,181 --> 00:06:57,548
Mr. Page? PAGE: Yeah.

103
00:06:57,717 --> 00:07:00,778
Uh, Lt. Rutledge, Homicide.

104
00:07:01,087 --> 00:07:03,488
I understand you were married
to the victim once. That right?

105
00:07:03,657 --> 00:07:05,125
Yeah. For two years.

106
00:07:06,293 --> 00:07:09,559
Uh, she took a quick trip
to Haiti, and that was that.

107
00:07:09,729 --> 00:07:12,096
What were you doing
at the concert tonight?

108
00:07:12,265 --> 00:07:14,894
I was recording it.
I'm still her producer.

109
00:07:15,068 --> 00:07:17,128
We were, uh,
cutting a live album.

110
00:07:17,304 --> 00:07:20,638
So, uh, things between
you were amicable?

111
00:07:20,807 --> 00:07:22,207
When she was sober.

112
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:25,368
- She sober tonight?
- Fair.

113
00:07:25,545 --> 00:07:28,105
I mean, she always drank
when she was doing a gig.

114
00:07:28,281 --> 00:07:30,079
Rum and diet cola.

115
00:07:30,250 --> 00:07:32,412
You, uh, talk to her
after the show tonight?

116
00:07:32,585 --> 00:07:35,714
No. The only time I talked to
her was during sound check.

117
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:38,454
- Talk to anybody after the show?
- Yeah.

118
00:07:38,625 --> 00:07:41,254
I talked to, you know,
my crew and my engineer,

119
00:07:41,428 --> 00:07:44,626
and, um, K.C.'s
manager, Steve Fisher.

120
00:07:44,798 --> 00:07:46,358
<i>K. C. asked for a
rough mix of the show.</i>

121
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:48,506
<i>So I was sitting at the
console, mixing some tracks,</i>

122
00:07:48,635 --> 00:07:50,536
<i>and he came storming by.</i>

123
00:07:50,937 --> 00:07:54,465
- Steve, what's wrong?
- She fired me.

124
00:07:54,641 --> 00:07:57,270
- Oh, man, are you kidding me?
- No. I'm history.

125
00:07:57,978 --> 00:07:59,674
- What did you do?
- What did I do?

126
00:07:59,846 --> 00:08:03,715
I made her a star, that's all, right
up there with Tanya and Wynonna.

127
00:08:03,883 --> 00:08:05,977
And how does she
repay me? She dumps me.

128
00:08:07,354 --> 00:08:11,553
Some agency in L.A. put a lot
of talk about specials and movies

129
00:08:11,725 --> 00:08:14,661
and commercials in her head.
She signed with them this morning.

130
00:08:15,295 --> 00:08:16,763
So, what are you gonna do?

131
00:08:18,631 --> 00:08:19,860
I don't know yet.

132
00:08:20,033 --> 00:08:21,433
Where'd he go after that?

133
00:08:21,601 --> 00:08:22,694
I have no idea.

134
00:08:23,269 --> 00:08:24,760
Where did you go after that?

135
00:08:24,938 --> 00:08:28,739
Well, I sat there for about 20
minutes, mixing those tracks,

136
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:33,243
<i>and, um, then I went to take
the tape to K. C. in the trailer,</i>

137
00:08:35,181 --> 00:08:37,650
<i>and the lights were on and
there was music coming out,</i>

138
00:08:37,817 --> 00:08:41,618
<i>so I, uh, knocked on the
door, and then I went in.</i>

139
00:08:51,231 --> 00:08:56,397
So I, um, went back in
inside, and I just started yelling.

140
00:08:56,569 --> 00:08:59,801
I guess I kind of
lost it a little bit.

141
00:08:59,973 --> 00:09:03,501
Was there anybody else out
back when you went by the trailer?

142
00:09:03,676 --> 00:09:05,838
No, I don't think so.

143
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:09,948
Do you happen to remember, uh,
when you were talking to Mr. Fisher

144
00:09:10,116 --> 00:09:14,554
whether or not he had a, uh, mark
or a stain on the sleeve of his shirt?

145
00:09:14,921 --> 00:09:16,412
No. I don't think so.

146
00:09:16,589 --> 00:09:17,613
Okay, Mr. Page.

147
00:09:17,791 --> 00:09:20,226
Uh, if I have any more
questions, I'll give you a call.

148
00:09:37,010 --> 00:09:40,447
"To Steve, your
pal, Randy Travis."

149
00:09:40,613 --> 00:09:42,741
- You know Randy Travis.
- Heh.

150
00:09:42,916 --> 00:09:44,908
Come on, Ben. Of
course. I know everybody.

151
00:09:45,085 --> 00:09:46,713
Hmm.

152
00:09:46,886 --> 00:09:50,789
- Why? Are you a fan of his?
- Yeah, heh.

153
00:09:51,357 --> 00:09:53,792
How'd you like to
have dinner with him?

154
00:09:54,127 --> 00:09:56,961
You can fix it so I'd have
dinner with Randy Travis?

155
00:09:57,130 --> 00:09:59,895
Hey, you take on
Jimmy Ray's case,

156
00:10:00,066 --> 00:10:03,730
and I'll see to it that you have
dinner with a different star every week.

157
00:10:03,903 --> 00:10:05,943
Sort of like belonging to
one of those record clubs.

158
00:10:06,072 --> 00:10:07,096
I don't know.

159
00:10:07,273 --> 00:10:09,499
I mean, I don't know

160
00:10:09,676 --> 00:10:12,271
- much about entertainment law.
- One minute, heh.

161
00:10:21,221 --> 00:10:23,261
Hello, again, Mr. Fisher.
Pat Rutledge, Atlanta P.D.

162
00:10:23,389 --> 00:10:24,549
- We spoke last night.
- Yeah.

163
00:10:24,691 --> 00:10:26,057
You got a minute?

164
00:10:26,226 --> 00:10:27,956
Well, as a matter of
fact, I've got company.

165
00:10:28,128 --> 00:10:31,997
This won't take long. I just
want you to roll up your sleeve.

166
00:10:33,399 --> 00:10:35,800
- The right one.
- What for?

167
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:38,234
Medical examiner found
skin tissue and dried blood

168
00:10:38,404 --> 00:10:40,100
beneath a couple of
Ms. West's fingernails.

169
00:10:40,273 --> 00:10:43,209
Figured, uh, she may have
scratched whoever killed her,

170
00:10:43,376 --> 00:10:46,608
and, well, since there was
something red on your sleeve last night,

171
00:10:47,113 --> 00:10:49,878
you can understand why we'd wanna
take a look, can't you, Mr. Fisher?

172
00:10:50,550 --> 00:10:51,950
What if I don't wanna do it?

173
00:10:52,118 --> 00:10:53,958
Well, we can do it here,
we can do it downtown,

174
00:10:54,120 --> 00:10:55,800
we can do it after we
get a search warrant,

175
00:10:55,955 --> 00:10:58,015
but, uh, we're gonna
look at that arm.

176
00:10:58,758 --> 00:11:00,283
Oh, yeah?

177
00:11:00,460 --> 00:11:03,658
Well, maybe we should just see
what my attorney says about it.

178
00:11:05,798 --> 00:11:07,790
- What?
- Come on, Ben. You gotta help me.

179
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:10,960
Oh, no, heh. I ju...

180
00:11:11,404 --> 00:11:13,669
Just name your fee.

181
00:11:20,947 --> 00:11:22,643
Hundred and fifty thousand.

182
00:11:22,815 --> 00:11:24,943
- Up front?
- Yep.

183
00:11:25,118 --> 00:11:27,019
And expenses.

184
00:11:27,187 --> 00:11:31,750
And dinner with, you
know, Randy Travis.

185
00:11:32,659 --> 00:11:34,287
Done.

186
00:11:36,095 --> 00:11:38,064
Show him your arm.

187
00:11:38,865 --> 00:11:40,629
Ben, I can't.

188
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:43,827
You have to. He
has probable cause.

189
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:01,781
I think you'd better
come with us, Mr. Fisher.

190
00:12:29,649 --> 00:12:31,584
Excuse me, ha, ha. Excuse me.

191
00:12:32,018 --> 00:12:35,182
Sorry. Got in your way
here with the picture there.

192
00:12:35,355 --> 00:12:36,846
- Hi, Lieutenant.
- Hi, Mr. Matlock.

193
00:12:37,023 --> 00:12:39,356
Yeah. There's not a lot of
room in these things, is there?

194
00:12:39,525 --> 00:12:40,652
No. Uh...

195
00:12:40,827 --> 00:12:43,427
Say, Mr. Matlock, listen, I know
you have the right to be in here,

196
00:12:43,563 --> 00:12:46,897
but, uh, my men are trying
to find the murder weapon.

197
00:12:47,066 --> 00:12:49,194
Do you mind stepping
outside till they're through?

198
00:12:49,369 --> 00:12:52,464
- Uh, well... You go with me?
- Okay.

199
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:55,399
Excuse me again, ha, ha.

200
00:13:01,547 --> 00:13:03,607
Ahem, you know
what you're looking for?

201
00:13:03,783 --> 00:13:05,979
Knife with a 6-inch
serrated blade.

202
00:13:06,152 --> 00:13:08,212
- Hunting knife.
- Probably.

203
00:13:08,388 --> 00:13:10,108
In fact, we think it
belonged to the victim.

204
00:13:10,256 --> 00:13:12,589
A couple of people remember
seeing it on a shelf in there.

205
00:13:12,759 --> 00:13:14,489
Think she got it as a
souvenir or something.

206
00:13:14,661 --> 00:13:16,129
It sure would save
us a lot of time

207
00:13:16,296 --> 00:13:18,663
if your client would just
tell us what he did with it.

208
00:13:18,831 --> 00:13:22,029
All you've got on my client is a
couple of scratches on his arm.

209
00:13:22,435 --> 00:13:23,835
Scratches he lied about.

210
00:13:24,237 --> 00:13:28,265
Lieutenant, we just found
this in the dumpster over there.

211
00:13:28,675 --> 00:13:29,938
"K.C."

212
00:13:30,109 --> 00:13:32,704
Looks like one of Ms.
West's handkerchiefs.

213
00:13:34,914 --> 00:13:36,815
<i>Don't care what you say</i>

214
00:13:36,983 --> 00:13:41,544
<i>On my way Better this way</i>

215
00:13:41,721 --> 00:13:44,384
How does he explain
those scratches on his arm?

216
00:13:44,557 --> 00:13:48,119
Well, he said he and K.C.
had an argument backstage,

217
00:13:48,294 --> 00:13:49,854
and she slapped him,
and he grabbed her,

218
00:13:49,996 --> 00:13:52,591
and she scratched his
arm, and he turned her loose,

219
00:13:52,765 --> 00:13:54,996
and she went stomping
out to her trailer.

220
00:13:55,168 --> 00:13:56,192
<i>Better this way</i>

221
00:13:56,369 --> 00:13:58,964
- Did he go after her?
- Said he didn't.

222
00:14:00,606 --> 00:14:04,441
And you believe him because
he paid you $150,000 up front.

223
00:14:05,178 --> 00:14:07,170
That, and he knows Randy Travis.

224
00:14:12,885 --> 00:14:15,211
<i>Better this way</i>

225
00:14:15,388 --> 00:14:19,621
<i>I'm on my way
It's better this way</i>

226
00:14:28,501 --> 00:14:30,094
Hi, Benjamin.

227
00:14:31,003 --> 00:14:34,405
- Hello, Bunny.
- Bunny?

228
00:14:34,574 --> 00:14:36,167
Ms. DuBois.

229
00:14:36,342 --> 00:14:41,371
- Bunny. And you're...?
- Leanne Mclntyre.

230
00:14:41,681 --> 00:14:44,150
- Benjamin's daughter.
- Heh.

231
00:14:44,317 --> 00:14:48,049
- Benjamin and I are very...
- Old acquaintances.

232
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:50,656
Watch who you're calling old.

233
00:14:50,823 --> 00:14:52,792
Benjamin, aren't you
gonna invite me in?

234
00:14:54,594 --> 00:14:56,756
Oh, sure, Bunny. I'm sorry.

235
00:14:56,929 --> 00:15:00,491
- Won't you, uh, come in?
- Thank you.

236
00:15:13,079 --> 00:15:16,015
- Well, you look good.
- Thank you, Bunny.

237
00:15:16,182 --> 00:15:20,050
You, uh... You look good, too.

238
00:15:22,922 --> 00:15:24,515
What can I do for you?

239
00:15:24,690 --> 00:15:26,330
Twenty-five years
in the business,

240
00:15:26,759 --> 00:15:30,059
without as much as a
parking ticket, and now this.

241
00:15:30,229 --> 00:15:32,255
- Now what?
- You're the lawyer, Benjamin.

242
00:15:32,432 --> 00:15:33,661
You explain it to her.

243
00:15:33,833 --> 00:15:36,064
Actually, I'm a
lawyer too. You mind?

244
00:15:36,235 --> 00:15:39,763
I can... Twenty-five
years, and there I was,

245
00:15:39,939 --> 00:15:42,932
being handcuffed and hauled
downtown and fingerprinted.

246
00:15:43,109 --> 00:15:44,873
It was mortifying.

247
00:15:45,945 --> 00:15:48,574
- Solicitation.
- Entrapment.

248
00:15:48,748 --> 00:15:51,912
And, believe me, this is something
I'm not going to take lying down.

249
00:16:12,405 --> 00:16:13,566
Hi, Steve.

250
00:16:13,739 --> 00:16:16,538
Listen, I don't think I should answer
any questions without my lawyer.

251
00:16:16,709 --> 00:16:17,802
No questions.

252
00:16:17,977 --> 00:16:20,003
I've a duly authorized
warrant to search your condo

253
00:16:20,179 --> 00:16:21,442
for the murder weapon.

254
00:16:21,614 --> 00:16:23,494
You can call your lawyer,
if you want. Go ahead.

255
00:16:23,649 --> 00:16:25,174
How about I go upstairs?

256
00:16:25,351 --> 00:16:27,513
Ugh, I didn't kill her.

257
00:16:27,687 --> 00:16:30,555
Maybe you blocked it
out. It happens sometimes.

258
00:16:30,723 --> 00:16:34,182
Why don't you look at these?
Maybe you'll remember something.

259
00:16:47,206 --> 00:16:48,799
You know what, Steve?

260
00:16:48,975 --> 00:16:51,809
We catch a lot of
killers. You know why?

261
00:16:51,978 --> 00:16:53,071
They make mistakes.

262
00:16:53,246 --> 00:16:56,478
You know what? You're sick.

263
00:17:16,769 --> 00:17:18,670
We'll be done in a
couple of minutes.

264
00:17:18,838 --> 00:17:20,067
The knife's not here.

265
00:17:20,239 --> 00:17:22,799
Uh, we're just dotting
the I's, crossing the T's.

266
00:17:22,975 --> 00:17:25,001
We don't really need it.

267
00:17:32,151 --> 00:17:35,713
<i>I know someday I'll be okay</i>

268
00:17:35,888 --> 00:17:40,223
<i>It's better this way</i> Uh...

269
00:17:41,294 --> 00:17:44,255
<i>Yeah</i> The, um...

270
00:17:44,430 --> 00:17:47,696
The bass is still too hot,
especially on the verses, and, um...

271
00:17:47,867 --> 00:17:48,891
No, I changed my mind.

272
00:17:49,068 --> 00:17:51,547
I don't like those, uh, fills on
the turnaround. We're gonna have

273
00:17:51,571 --> 00:17:54,598
- to work something out. Um...
- I'll take care of it.

274
00:17:54,774 --> 00:17:56,504
- What's up?
- So, what are you working on?

275
00:17:56,676 --> 00:17:58,110
K.C.'s last album.

276
00:17:58,277 --> 00:18:00,769
It's a rush job,
live and in concert.

277
00:18:00,947 --> 00:18:03,781
The, uh, record company is
trying to cash in on the publicity.

278
00:18:03,950 --> 00:18:06,146
Oh, well...

279
00:18:06,586 --> 00:18:09,055
- What can I do for you?
- Oh, well, uh,

280
00:18:09,221 --> 00:18:13,283
somebody saw you backstage,
uh, the night of the concert

281
00:18:13,459 --> 00:18:17,624
and said you and K.C. had
an argument before the show.

282
00:18:17,797 --> 00:18:20,892
Oh, that wasn't an argument. That
was, heh... That was a discussion.

283
00:18:21,067 --> 00:18:23,093
That was, you know,
an exchange of ideas

284
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:26,671
between two fairly
vocal people. That's all.

285
00:18:26,839 --> 00:18:29,104
I am not dropping
the keyboard bit.

286
00:18:29,275 --> 00:18:32,370
It's practically the best number
in the show. The audience loves it.

287
00:18:32,545 --> 00:18:34,776
I cannot mike it properly, K.C.

288
00:18:34,947 --> 00:18:36,506
I am trying to record
an album here.

289
00:18:36,682 --> 00:18:38,913
Well, that is your problem.

290
00:18:39,085 --> 00:18:41,213
Seeing that these people
get their 30 bucks worth,

291
00:18:41,387 --> 00:18:44,152
which means that me and
Dusty are doing that duet.

292
00:18:44,323 --> 00:18:46,656
- Then it won't be on the album.
- Come on, John.

293
00:18:46,826 --> 00:18:48,692
Figure something out.

294
00:18:48,861 --> 00:18:52,628
- You always do.
- Not this time, I can't.

295
00:18:52,798 --> 00:18:54,528
But it's my favorite number.

296
00:18:57,670 --> 00:19:02,233
Then do it. Just leave the
routine with Dusty out. That's all.

297
00:19:02,408 --> 00:19:05,537
I've been doing the number
the same way for three months.

298
00:19:06,045 --> 00:19:08,947
I can't just do it different
and then screw up.

299
00:19:09,115 --> 00:19:11,744
I can't change horses
midstream like that.

300
00:19:11,917 --> 00:19:13,613
Oh, sure, you can, honey.

301
00:19:13,786 --> 00:19:15,277
You dumped me, didn't you?

302
00:19:19,792 --> 00:19:21,954
You can do it, Kase.

303
00:19:22,495 --> 00:19:23,519
All right, sweetheart?

304
00:19:23,996 --> 00:19:25,794
<i>So, uh, why did she dump you?</i>

305
00:19:25,965 --> 00:19:28,025
Now, that's a long story.

306
00:19:28,200 --> 00:19:32,069
Because, uh, you
like to play the ponies?

307
00:19:32,838 --> 00:19:34,739
I suppose that was
probably part of it.

308
00:19:34,907 --> 00:19:37,570
That, uh, $100,000
insurance policy

309
00:19:37,743 --> 00:19:39,803
you took out on her
about six months ago,

310
00:19:39,979 --> 00:19:41,899
was that because you
like to play the ponies too?

311
00:19:43,049 --> 00:19:45,075
- What do you mean?
- Well,

312
00:19:45,251 --> 00:19:46,611
a fella that does
as much business

313
00:19:46,752 --> 00:19:48,380
with bookies and
loan sharks as you do,

314
00:19:48,554 --> 00:19:50,352
a hundred thousand
dollars come in handy.

315
00:19:50,523 --> 00:19:53,493
That would just be a drop
in the bucket, Mr. Matlock.

316
00:19:53,659 --> 00:19:56,959
This. This is how I keep
those guys off my back.

317
00:19:57,129 --> 00:19:58,324
It's the music of K.C. West.

318
00:19:58,497 --> 00:20:01,797
This is a multimillion-dollar
gravy train.

319
00:20:01,967 --> 00:20:04,402
And I'd be the last guy on
earth to derail it, believe me.

320
00:20:04,570 --> 00:20:07,062
- Could I...? Could I look at...?
- Yeah, sure.

321
00:20:07,773 --> 00:20:09,298
Hmm...

322
00:20:09,975 --> 00:20:11,841
I don't, uh...

323
00:20:12,445 --> 00:20:15,574
Ugh, I don't have this
one. I bet it's pretty.

324
00:20:16,048 --> 00:20:17,880
- Be my guest.
- Prob...

325
00:20:19,118 --> 00:20:20,609
Oh.

326
00:20:21,687 --> 00:20:22,780
Oh, thank you.

327
00:20:27,359 --> 00:20:30,386
<i>It's better that way
That's what I say</i>

328
00:20:30,563 --> 00:20:32,828
<i>Better that way</i>

329
00:20:37,937 --> 00:20:40,304
- John.
- Hey, Steve, how you doing?

330
00:20:40,473 --> 00:20:44,501
- I was, uh, just talking to your lawyer.
- Yeah, I know.

331
00:20:44,677 --> 00:20:47,579
Hey, look, man, I got a
favor to ask. A big favor.

332
00:20:47,747 --> 00:20:48,771
Sure.

333
00:20:49,548 --> 00:20:52,382
I need you to get something
out of K.C.'s trailer for me.

334
00:20:52,551 --> 00:20:54,213
A pen. It's a fountain pen.

335
00:20:54,386 --> 00:20:57,413
I can't let the police find it. If they
do, they could use it against me.

336
00:20:57,590 --> 00:21:00,150
Fountain pen? What's a
fountain pen gonna prove?

337
00:21:00,326 --> 00:21:02,886
That afternoon, I signed
a contract with Brian Levy.

338
00:21:03,062 --> 00:21:04,086
- You know him.
- Yeah.

339
00:21:04,263 --> 00:21:06,494
- The, uh, record promoter.
- Right.

340
00:21:06,665 --> 00:21:11,467
Anyway, he gives me the pen that
I use to sign the contract as a gift.

341
00:21:11,637 --> 00:21:13,765
- It's got his name on it.
- Oh.

342
00:21:13,939 --> 00:21:17,535
Anyway, that pen somehow
found its way into K.C.'s trailer,

343
00:21:17,710 --> 00:21:19,910
and if the police find the
pen, they talk to Brian Levy,

344
00:21:20,045 --> 00:21:23,209
they're gonna know that I'd
just gotten the damn thing.

345
00:21:23,382 --> 00:21:26,302
And they're gonna say that the only
way that it could have gotten in there

346
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:29,547
is if I dropped it that night in
K.C.'s trailer when I was killing her.

347
00:21:30,322 --> 00:21:31,722
How did it get in there?

348
00:21:32,825 --> 00:21:35,056
I don't know. I swear to God.

349
00:21:35,227 --> 00:21:36,752
Why don't you
go get it yourself?

350
00:21:36,929 --> 00:21:39,694
Because the police
taped the trailer shut.

351
00:21:39,865 --> 00:21:41,424
If they catch me
sneaking around there,

352
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:43,193
things are gonna
really look bad for me.

353
00:21:43,369 --> 00:21:45,849
I don't want them to catch me
sneaking around in there, either.

354
00:21:46,005 --> 00:21:47,598
- Come on, John.
- No.

355
00:21:47,773 --> 00:21:50,373
I won't do it. That would be
breaking and entering. I won't do it.

356
00:21:50,810 --> 00:21:55,612
It would be doing me a big favor
that I would never ever forget.

357
00:21:56,715 --> 00:21:59,514
Come on. I can get you a key.

358
00:22:54,607 --> 00:22:57,567
Is this the pen you tried to remove
from the decedent's trailer that night,

359
00:22:57,710 --> 00:22:59,804
- Mr. Page?
- Yes, it is.

360
00:22:59,979 --> 00:23:01,311
Why did you try to remove it?

361
00:23:01,914 --> 00:23:03,382
Steve Fisher asked me to.

362
00:23:03,782 --> 00:23:05,910
Did he tell you
why he wanted it?

363
00:23:07,653 --> 00:23:08,916
Oh, come on now, Mr. Page.

364
00:23:09,088 --> 00:23:11,922
You had to break through a
police seal to get into that trailer.

365
00:23:12,391 --> 00:23:15,793
You knew you were breaking
the law. But you did it, anyway.

366
00:23:16,228 --> 00:23:17,753
Now, why?

367
00:23:20,165 --> 00:23:22,285
Steve thought that if the
cops found the pen in there,

368
00:23:22,434 --> 00:23:24,164
they'd say that that
proved he killed her.

369
00:23:24,336 --> 00:23:26,496
Which he didn't do. I have
known Steve for a long time.

370
00:23:26,639 --> 00:23:29,079
- He wouldn't kill anybody.
- That's all. Thank you, Mr. Page.

371
00:23:49,161 --> 00:23:51,630
Come on, Ben, say something.

372
00:23:51,797 --> 00:23:53,390
You don't wanna hear it.

373
00:23:53,565 --> 00:23:55,090
Hey, you don't think I'm guilty.

374
00:23:55,267 --> 00:24:00,171
I think you're a bona fide, card-carrying
jackass who ought to be jailed,

375
00:24:00,339 --> 00:24:04,504
if for no other reason, to
keep you from reproducing.

376
00:24:04,677 --> 00:24:07,112
Why didn't you come to
me about that fountain pen?

377
00:24:07,279 --> 00:24:09,441
- You would have stolen it for me?
- No.

378
00:24:09,615 --> 00:24:14,747
I have to know the facts, all
the facts, if I'm gonna defend you.

379
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:16,821
Is there something
else you haven't told me?

380
00:24:16,989 --> 00:24:20,585
Oh, man, you gonna get mad.

381
00:24:21,327 --> 00:24:24,354
I did go to her
trailer that night.

382
00:24:24,530 --> 00:24:26,863
- Oh, great.
- Ugh.

383
00:24:27,299 --> 00:24:28,597
Let's hear it.

384
00:24:28,767 --> 00:24:32,260
After we had our little altercation,
I went up the aisle into the lobby.

385
00:24:32,438 --> 00:24:35,670
I hit the front door and
I just started walking.

386
00:24:35,841 --> 00:24:37,810
Only, the longer I
walked, the madder I got,

387
00:24:37,977 --> 00:24:41,345
until, finally, I just turned
around and I came back.

388
00:24:49,088 --> 00:24:50,920
K.C.

389
00:25:09,808 --> 00:25:12,004
Listen, I didn't tell the police

390
00:25:12,177 --> 00:25:15,636
for the same reason as I didn't
tell you about the fountain pen.

391
00:25:15,814 --> 00:25:18,283
I was afraid of
how it would look.

392
00:25:18,450 --> 00:25:20,112
I was there. But
I didn't kill her.

393
00:25:20,285 --> 00:25:22,085
And I couldn't have
dropped that fountain pen.

394
00:25:22,221 --> 00:25:23,553
I wasn't there long enough.

395
00:25:28,060 --> 00:25:29,528
Ben?

396
00:25:32,231 --> 00:25:34,223
- You saw the knife?
- Yeah.

397
00:25:34,400 --> 00:25:37,097
It was lying right
there next to her body.

398
00:25:37,536 --> 00:25:41,132
- Police couldn't find it.
- I know.

399
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:43,060
Kind of bothered me
when I heard about that,

400
00:25:43,208 --> 00:25:45,143
but I was in no
position to say anything.

401
00:25:46,211 --> 00:25:48,737
Well, if you saw the knife,

402
00:25:48,914 --> 00:25:53,716
and it wasn't there when
the police arrived, where is it?

403
00:27:11,830 --> 00:27:13,765
Ms. DuBois.

404
00:27:14,433 --> 00:27:18,336
- So where is the silver fox?
- Uh, he'll be here.

405
00:27:20,606 --> 00:27:22,598
- Miss DuBois.
- Bunny.

406
00:27:22,774 --> 00:27:24,868
Bunny. I was wondering.

407
00:27:25,043 --> 00:27:26,978
How well do you know my father?

408
00:27:27,146 --> 00:27:31,049
- And how do you know my father?
- Heh.

409
00:27:31,216 --> 00:27:32,684
Well, uh... Oh.

410
00:27:33,719 --> 00:27:36,587
Hi. I'm sorry I'm
late. I miss anything?

411
00:27:36,755 --> 00:27:39,486
Case Number 1047B3.

412
00:27:39,658 --> 00:27:42,457
<i>The State of Georgia
v. Beverly DuBois.</i>

413
00:27:42,628 --> 00:27:44,893
I guess not. Come on.

414
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:52,762
Mr. Matlock, has the state provided
you with the information you requested

415
00:27:52,938 --> 00:27:54,236
in your pre-trial motions?

416
00:27:54,840 --> 00:27:55,864
Yes, sir.

417
00:27:56,041 --> 00:27:59,534
Then I shall begin hearing
arguments at 10 a.m.

418
00:27:59,711 --> 00:28:02,840
on the 29th in this courtroom.

419
00:28:03,015 --> 00:28:05,484
Is that okay with everyone?

420
00:28:05,684 --> 00:28:11,248
Yes, sir. Ten a.m.,
29th, in this courtroom.

421
00:28:36,949 --> 00:28:39,111
Ugh, what?

422
00:28:39,284 --> 00:28:41,879
I just found this in my car.

423
00:28:42,554 --> 00:28:46,616
I swear to you, Ben, I have
no idea how it got there.

424
00:28:47,626 --> 00:28:49,993
- Steve.
- Yeah.

425
00:28:50,162 --> 00:28:52,324
Close the door.

426
00:28:54,066 --> 00:28:56,592
It was jammed under the
backrest of the passenger seat.

427
00:28:56,768 --> 00:29:00,102
Somebody must have broke
into my car and put it there.

428
00:29:01,506 --> 00:29:04,476
- Did you call the police?
- Of course not.

429
00:29:04,643 --> 00:29:06,168
Hmm.

430
00:29:09,014 --> 00:29:10,482
- Who are you calling?
- The police.

431
00:29:10,649 --> 00:29:12,675
No. You can't do that.

432
00:29:12,851 --> 00:29:16,288
- This could be the murder weapon.
- It is the murder weapon. Believe me.

433
00:29:16,455 --> 00:29:20,290
- It was lying right next to K.C.'s body.
- I can't withhold evidence.

434
00:29:20,459 --> 00:29:23,293
Why not? I'm paying you a hundred
and fifty grand, for crying out loud.

435
00:29:23,462 --> 00:29:25,260
Get your finger off my phone.

436
00:29:32,004 --> 00:29:34,124
I should have just tossed
the damn thing off a bridge.

437
00:29:34,740 --> 00:29:35,935
I'm surprised you didn't.

438
00:29:36,108 --> 00:29:38,600
I figured the way my luck was
running, it would have floated.

439
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:41,471
It's a frame, Ben.

440
00:29:41,647 --> 00:29:43,673
First, the pen, now this.

441
00:29:43,849 --> 00:29:48,651
Somebody's not only trying to get
away with murder, they're trying to get me.

442
00:29:49,655 --> 00:29:51,455
<i>Atlanta Police Department.</i>

443
00:29:53,859 --> 00:29:56,351
<i>Hello? Atlanta
Police Department.</i>

444
00:30:03,101 --> 00:30:10,133
Uh, Lieutenant, would you, uh,
identify these pictures for us, please?

445
00:30:10,442 --> 00:30:13,310
They're the pictures the police
photographer took of the crime scene.

446
00:30:13,478 --> 00:30:16,346
Mm-hm. And what
purpose do they serve?

447
00:30:16,515 --> 00:30:18,916
They record the position
and condition of the corpse,

448
00:30:19,084 --> 00:30:24,022
as well as the exact location of
any objects in the immediate vicinity

449
00:30:24,456 --> 00:30:27,255
which may or may not prove
relevant to our investigation.

450
00:30:27,426 --> 00:30:32,091
This pen here, the picture of the
pen, uh, proved real relevant, didn't it?

451
00:30:32,831 --> 00:30:33,890
Yes, sir, it did.

452
00:30:35,600 --> 00:30:37,501
You know what's interesting,

453
00:30:37,669 --> 00:30:41,800
is this pen only appears
in one of these pictures.

454
00:30:41,973 --> 00:30:43,100
Why is that?

455
00:30:43,642 --> 00:30:44,962
Well, I guess it
was only visible

456
00:30:45,110 --> 00:30:46,669
- from that one angle.
- Yeah. Yeah.

457
00:30:46,845 --> 00:30:51,112
And more interesting, the police
photographer who took these pictures

458
00:30:51,283 --> 00:30:54,913
says that the negative for
this picture, and this one only,

459
00:30:55,087 --> 00:30:57,886
is missing from his
files. Why is that?

460
00:30:59,057 --> 00:31:00,457
I have no idea.

461
00:31:00,625 --> 00:31:03,618
And what's even more interesting

462
00:31:03,795 --> 00:31:08,096
is that all of these negatives

463
00:31:08,266 --> 00:31:12,533
are in sequence from
one to 31. See that?

464
00:31:13,572 --> 00:31:16,804
The rest of the roll
was never exposed.

465
00:31:16,975 --> 00:31:20,571
See? Thirty-two to 36.

466
00:31:20,746 --> 00:31:21,873
Blank.

467
00:31:22,047 --> 00:31:25,017
So where did this
picture come from?

468
00:31:25,951 --> 00:31:27,442
- I don't know.
- Is it possible

469
00:31:27,619 --> 00:31:31,112
that someone could have taken this
picture, using a different roll of film,

470
00:31:31,289 --> 00:31:32,951
maybe even a different camera?

471
00:31:33,358 --> 00:31:35,589
No. The area was
cordoned off and sealed.

472
00:31:35,761 --> 00:31:39,596
Is it possible that someone
simply shuffled a copy of this picture

473
00:31:39,765 --> 00:31:43,361
in with the other 10 sets that
were made and distributed?

474
00:31:43,969 --> 00:31:45,164
No.

475
00:31:45,337 --> 00:31:46,635
No? Why not?

476
00:31:46,805 --> 00:31:48,845
Only someone in the
department could have done that,

477
00:31:48,974 --> 00:31:51,603
- and, I assure you, that's not the case.
- No?

478
00:31:51,777 --> 00:31:56,613
Suppose I told you that
someone within the department

479
00:31:56,782 --> 00:32:00,651
not only was at K.C.
West's concert that night,

480
00:32:00,819 --> 00:32:03,846
but was the first
one at the scene.

481
00:32:04,322 --> 00:32:07,588
Not only spearheaded
the investigation,

482
00:32:07,759 --> 00:32:11,696
but was responsible for the
distribution of these pictures.

483
00:32:12,998 --> 00:32:15,524
What would you say to
that, Lieutenant Rutledge?

484
00:32:18,036 --> 00:32:20,938
Not much, because you were
that someone, weren't you?

485
00:32:22,574 --> 00:32:25,305
In the course of your
preliminary interviews,

486
00:32:25,477 --> 00:32:30,040
you found out that someone
gave my client a pen that afternoon.

487
00:32:30,215 --> 00:32:34,243
So you stole it, put it in
K.C.'s trailer, took a picture of it.

488
00:32:34,419 --> 00:32:35,443
That's ridiculous.

489
00:32:35,620 --> 00:32:38,215
Then you waved it in
front of my client's face

490
00:32:38,390 --> 00:32:41,849
in hopes he'd do something
dumb to further incriminate himself.

491
00:32:42,027 --> 00:32:43,518
Which he did.

492
00:32:44,095 --> 00:32:48,260
Then you planted the
murder weapon in his car

493
00:32:48,433 --> 00:32:52,871
- for even more evidence against him.
- No. No. Absolutely not.

494
00:32:53,038 --> 00:32:57,305
The only question that
remains is: Why'd you kill her?

495
00:32:58,043 --> 00:33:00,740
I didn't kill her. It was him.

496
00:33:03,482 --> 00:33:05,348
He killed her.

497
00:33:06,718 --> 00:33:08,949
He started controlling
her four years ago

498
00:33:09,120 --> 00:33:15,356
and slowly, day-by-day, inch-by-inch,
show-by-show he destroyed her.

499
00:33:15,527 --> 00:33:17,553
You got a real future.

500
00:33:18,296 --> 00:33:19,821
Way to go, Barry.

501
00:33:24,503 --> 00:33:25,743
What'd that guy want?

502
00:33:25,871 --> 00:33:27,669
- Uh, he's an artists' manager.
- Uh-huh.

503
00:33:27,839 --> 00:33:30,900
He's got an office in
Nashville. He liked me.

504
00:33:31,076 --> 00:33:32,100
Yeah. Why wouldn't he?

505
00:33:32,277 --> 00:33:34,746
Well, I mean, this could be it.

506
00:33:34,913 --> 00:33:37,849
Remember, that's a business card,
it's not a contract. Words are cheap.

507
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:39,314
Come on.

508
00:33:39,484 --> 00:33:41,749
Are all officers so damn
suspicious of everybody?

509
00:33:41,920 --> 00:33:43,912
Mostly just me.

510
00:33:44,089 --> 00:33:46,149
Whoo!

511
00:33:49,027 --> 00:33:50,928
I loved her.

512
00:33:53,798 --> 00:33:55,926
And he changed her.

513
00:33:57,969 --> 00:34:01,462
Her name, her hair, her clothes.

514
00:34:03,008 --> 00:34:06,445
And that's nothing compared
to what he did to her inside.

515
00:34:07,579 --> 00:34:10,048
Made her mean and selfish.

516
00:34:10,215 --> 00:34:14,949
Turned her into a drunk so she'd
forget about everybody but herself.

517
00:34:18,256 --> 00:34:20,657
I remember how she was.

518
00:34:24,763 --> 00:34:27,528
He may have changed her,

519
00:34:27,699 --> 00:34:29,361
- but you killed her.
- No.

520
00:34:29,534 --> 00:34:31,503
You went into her
trailer that night,

521
00:34:32,003 --> 00:34:34,734
confronted her with four
years of hurt and anger,

522
00:34:34,906 --> 00:34:36,169
then you stabbed her to death.

523
00:34:36,341 --> 00:34:38,333
I couldn't have
killed her. I loved her.

524
00:34:38,510 --> 00:34:40,138
I've always loved her.

525
00:34:40,312 --> 00:34:44,215
- Then you planted the evidence.
- I wanted to see him punished.

526
00:34:44,382 --> 00:34:45,406
Lieutenant,

527
00:34:45,584 --> 00:34:49,214
the officers who
answered the 911 call

528
00:34:49,387 --> 00:34:55,327
reported that you were already
at the scene when they arrived.

529
00:34:55,493 --> 00:34:57,155
How do you explain that?

530
00:34:59,631 --> 00:35:04,194
After the concert, I went to the
bar across the street, had a beer.

531
00:35:04,669 --> 00:35:06,262
Around 11:00,

532
00:35:06,438 --> 00:35:10,967
one of the stagehands came in
and said, uh, K.C. West was dead.

533
00:35:14,512 --> 00:35:16,140
I rushed over.

534
00:35:18,016 --> 00:35:21,646
- Anybody see you at the bar?
- Lots of people.

535
00:35:21,820 --> 00:35:25,018
Place was packed. I was there,
Mr. Matlock, you ask anybody.

536
00:35:34,165 --> 00:35:35,690
No further questions.

537
00:35:37,068 --> 00:35:39,162
Bailiff, remove this
man from my courtroom.

538
00:35:39,337 --> 00:35:43,968
In the meantime, this court is in
recess till 9 a.m. tomorrow morning,

539
00:35:44,142 --> 00:35:47,203
at which time I will
entertain any and all motions.

540
00:35:49,681 --> 00:35:51,809
What's going on, Ben?

541
00:35:51,983 --> 00:35:54,248
What does this all mean?

542
00:36:01,926 --> 00:36:04,157
K.C. <i>This way</i>

543
00:36:06,998 --> 00:36:10,763
<i>Yeah It's better this way</i>

544
00:36:10,935 --> 00:36:14,667
Well, sometimes it
doesn't pay to get up.

545
00:36:14,839 --> 00:36:17,206
Pat Rutledge had an alibi, heh.

546
00:36:17,375 --> 00:36:19,173
So what? He tampered
with the evidence.

547
00:36:19,344 --> 00:36:21,142
You're bound to get a mistrial.

548
00:36:21,312 --> 00:36:22,803
Might even get
the case dismissed.

549
00:36:22,981 --> 00:36:26,975
Well, I was sure he had done it.

550
00:36:27,385 --> 00:36:30,287
- Is that your tape in there or mine?
- Um...

551
00:36:30,455 --> 00:36:32,447
- You like K.C. West?
- Well, I do now.

552
00:36:32,624 --> 00:36:34,320
You played her so
much, she grew on me.

553
00:36:34,492 --> 00:36:37,257
Had to go out and buy my own.

554
00:36:38,463 --> 00:36:39,863
Oh.

555
00:36:40,165 --> 00:36:42,634
- Mine was free.
- Figures.

556
00:36:42,801 --> 00:36:44,326
Yeah.

557
00:36:45,603 --> 00:36:51,008
Not to worry. Dad, some
murder cases never get solved.

558
00:36:51,376 --> 00:36:53,936
- I'll see you later.
- Uh-huh.

559
00:36:55,680 --> 00:36:59,674
Yeah. Some don't. Some do.

560
00:37:05,924 --> 00:37:09,622
This court is now in
session. Be seated.

561
00:37:12,430 --> 00:37:15,059
Mr. Matlock, let's hear it.

562
00:37:15,233 --> 00:37:18,601
Your Honor, I know that
you and everybody else here

563
00:37:18,770 --> 00:37:22,229
kind of expects me
to move for a mistrial.

564
00:37:22,407 --> 00:37:25,571
But, first, I'd like to
call one last witness.

565
00:37:25,744 --> 00:37:26,871
Why?

566
00:37:27,045 --> 00:37:29,742
So justice can be
served, Your Honor.

567
00:37:29,914 --> 00:37:33,442
I believe the people who
loved K.C. West's music

568
00:37:33,618 --> 00:37:35,780
deserve to know why it ended.

569
00:37:36,221 --> 00:37:37,280
Proceed.

570
00:37:37,455 --> 00:37:40,721
Defense calls John Page.

571
00:37:40,892 --> 00:37:46,195
Uh, we've all heard of
piracy in the music business.

572
00:37:46,364 --> 00:37:47,957
Now, how does that work?

573
00:37:48,133 --> 00:37:50,659
Say, uh, piracy in
the cassette business.

574
00:37:50,835 --> 00:37:52,804
- Could you tell us about that?
- Sure.

575
00:37:52,971 --> 00:37:55,065
Um, somebody gets a hold
of the master of an album

576
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:57,266
and, uh, they
make an illicit copy

577
00:37:57,442 --> 00:37:59,035
and then they, uh,
dub it onto cassettes

578
00:37:59,210 --> 00:38:02,840
using these high-speed
machines called slaves,

579
00:38:03,014 --> 00:38:04,915
and then they, uh,
sell them to retailers

580
00:38:05,083 --> 00:38:07,552
who may or may not know
that they're buying bootleg tapes.

581
00:38:07,719 --> 00:38:12,180
Yeah. Yeah, well, these
cassettes have distinctive covers.

582
00:38:12,357 --> 00:38:16,453
Uh... Do they make
illicit copies of those too?

583
00:38:16,628 --> 00:38:20,030
Well, they could. But usually they,
uh, have somebody copy them by hand

584
00:38:20,198 --> 00:38:22,895
and then they, uh, run
fakes from the copy.

585
00:38:23,067 --> 00:38:25,832
You see, with cassettes, the
things is that they're so small,

586
00:38:26,004 --> 00:38:27,905
it's difficult to see any
detail in the artwork.

587
00:38:28,072 --> 00:38:30,632
It's, uh, quite hard to tell the
real stuff from the bogus stuff.

588
00:38:30,708 --> 00:38:32,734
Oh, yeah, I can see that.

589
00:38:32,911 --> 00:38:34,743
But you seem to
know a lot about this.

590
00:38:35,213 --> 00:38:36,933
I've been a record
producer for seven years,

591
00:38:37,081 --> 00:38:39,414
and I was a sound engineer
for seven years before that.

592
00:38:39,584 --> 00:38:42,144
I've seen just about everything
the music business has to offer.

593
00:38:42,654 --> 00:38:44,680
- Good and bad.
- Good and bad. Yeah.

594
00:38:44,856 --> 00:38:51,353
Uh, do you remember the cassette
you gave me of that K.C. West album?

595
00:38:51,529 --> 00:38:52,588
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

596
00:38:53,531 --> 00:38:56,091
You know, on the surface,

597
00:38:56,267 --> 00:39:02,366
it looks identical to the one my
daughter bought in a record store.

598
00:39:02,540 --> 00:39:03,564
See?

599
00:39:03,741 --> 00:39:05,209
But it's not.

600
00:39:05,376 --> 00:39:06,674
The store-bought one

601
00:39:06,845 --> 00:39:13,149
ahem, has the address of the
official K.C. West fan club on it.

602
00:39:13,318 --> 00:39:15,480
And the one you gave me doesn't.

603
00:39:16,387 --> 00:39:17,946
Why is that?

604
00:39:18,122 --> 00:39:20,717
I don't know. Maybe, you know,
they changed the packaging.

605
00:39:20,892 --> 00:39:23,225
No. No, no. I
called the printer,

606
00:39:23,862 --> 00:39:26,491
and she said the
fan club information

607
00:39:26,664 --> 00:39:28,860
was added to the
artwork at the last minute.

608
00:39:29,033 --> 00:39:30,524
But she guaranteed me

609
00:39:30,702 --> 00:39:34,798
that fan club information was
on every cover they printed.

610
00:39:34,973 --> 00:39:37,135
You know what that means?

611
00:39:38,009 --> 00:39:41,138
The cassette you
gave me is pirated.

612
00:39:41,312 --> 00:39:44,441
- Where'd you get it?
- I don't know.

613
00:39:44,616 --> 00:39:46,585
Sure you do, heh.

614
00:39:46,751 --> 00:39:49,744
You made it.
Secretly. Illegally.

615
00:39:49,921 --> 00:39:52,652
- And at great profit. Didn't you?
- No.

616
00:39:52,824 --> 00:39:54,417
And K.C. found out about it.

617
00:39:54,592 --> 00:39:56,925
So when you went
into her trailer that night,

618
00:39:57,095 --> 00:39:59,530
<i>she confronted you with it.</i>

619
00:39:59,697 --> 00:40:02,929
<i>Unfortunately, she didn't
know how much money</i>

620
00:40:03,101 --> 00:40:04,899
<i>you owed the loan sharks,</i>

621
00:40:05,069 --> 00:40:07,402
<i>how desperate
you were for money.</i>

622
00:40:07,572 --> 00:40:12,476
She didn't know she was
leaving you only one choice.

623
00:40:12,644 --> 00:40:14,510
You had to kill her. Didn't you?

624
00:40:14,946 --> 00:40:16,346
I didn't kill her.

625
00:40:16,514 --> 00:40:20,952
Now, I can't prove that
you're in the piracy business,

626
00:40:21,119 --> 00:40:25,215
but I can prove
you killed K.C. West.

627
00:40:27,058 --> 00:40:29,960
The day after the murder,

628
00:40:30,561 --> 00:40:35,625
the police found this in the
dumpster behind the concert hall.

629
00:40:35,800 --> 00:40:38,565
It's one of K.C.'s
handkerchiefs.

630
00:40:38,736 --> 00:40:42,173
There were bunches of
them all over her trailer.

631
00:40:42,340 --> 00:40:44,935
This one has blood on it.

632
00:40:45,710 --> 00:40:50,045
What was, uh, K.C.'s handkerchief
doing in that trash bin, Mr. Page?

633
00:40:50,214 --> 00:40:51,944
I have no idea.

634
00:40:52,116 --> 00:40:54,950
Think back to that
night. Just think back.

635
00:40:55,119 --> 00:40:59,989
Your fingerprints were all
over the handle of that knife.

636
00:41:00,158 --> 00:41:05,187
<i>So you took one of her
handkerchiefs and you wiped it clean,</i>

637
00:41:05,563 --> 00:41:09,330
<i>getting blood on the
handkerchief as a result.</i>

638
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:14,734
<i>So you placed the knife on the floor
by the body, rushed out of the trailer,</i>

639
00:41:14,906 --> 00:41:16,738
<i>threw the handkerchief
in the dumpster</i>

640
00:41:16,908 --> 00:41:19,309
on the way back
to the concert hall.

641
00:41:19,477 --> 00:41:22,003
And then, in a little while,
you went back to the trailer

642
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:28,279
as if nothing had happened
and discovered the body.

643
00:41:29,220 --> 00:41:31,416
- Right?
- No.

644
00:41:31,589 --> 00:41:32,921
That never happened.

645
00:41:37,028 --> 00:41:40,988
The police found these pants,

646
00:41:41,165 --> 00:41:44,693
removed them from your closet this
morning. With a warrant, of course.

647
00:41:44,869 --> 00:41:47,338
These are your
jeans, aren't they?

648
00:41:47,672 --> 00:41:49,664
- I guess so.
- Hmm.

649
00:41:49,841 --> 00:41:53,972
Uh, would you...? Would
you please, uh, pull out

650
00:41:54,145 --> 00:41:58,207
the, uh, right-hand
front pocket?

651
00:42:02,787 --> 00:42:04,585
Oh.

652
00:42:04,756 --> 00:42:07,157
Hmm...

653
00:42:10,395 --> 00:42:13,229
Lab tests prove that's blood.

654
00:42:13,698 --> 00:42:16,327
B-negative, same as K.C. West's.

655
00:42:16,501 --> 00:42:19,096
How'd this blood
get in the pocket?

656
00:42:22,840 --> 00:42:26,834
You couldn't take a chance
of walking into that parking lot,

657
00:42:27,011 --> 00:42:29,913
running into somebody out there.

658
00:42:30,314 --> 00:42:34,684
You couldn't be seen waving a
bloody handkerchief over your head.

659
00:42:34,852 --> 00:42:36,480
So you put it in your pocket,

660
00:42:36,888 --> 00:42:42,054
and that's how this
blood, K.C.'s blood,

661
00:42:42,226 --> 00:42:44,422
got in your pocket.

662
00:42:45,096 --> 00:42:47,725
Thanks to a policeman

663
00:42:48,466 --> 00:42:51,903
who tried to frame
the wrong man,

664
00:42:52,537 --> 00:42:54,904
and thanks to my client,

665
00:42:55,406 --> 00:43:01,744
who, out of the blue, asked you of
all people to steal that pen for him,

666
00:43:02,547 --> 00:43:04,743
thanks to them,

667
00:43:06,551 --> 00:43:11,353
you almost got away
with it, Mr. Page.

668
00:43:11,856 --> 00:43:13,916
Almost.

669
00:43:26,671 --> 00:43:28,196
- Hi. How'd it go?
- Oh.

670
00:43:28,372 --> 00:43:29,533
- Acquittal.
- Good.

671
00:43:29,707 --> 00:43:32,233
- Is, uh, Bunny here?
- Yeah, she's inside.

672
00:43:32,410 --> 00:43:35,005
Relax, Dad. Judge
Edwards isn't even here yet.

673
00:43:35,179 --> 00:43:38,274
I'm not really prepared on this
case. I don't feel good about it at all.

674
00:43:38,449 --> 00:43:39,940
I wouldn't worry about it.

675
00:43:40,118 --> 00:43:44,419
In fact, uh, 20 bucks says Judge
Edwards dismisses the case.

676
00:43:44,589 --> 00:43:48,924
Leanne, not a chance in hell.

677
00:43:49,560 --> 00:43:53,895
And since this is such a
clear-cut case of entrapment,

678
00:43:57,101 --> 00:43:58,194
I see no reason to waste

679
00:43:58,369 --> 00:44:01,271
anymore of anybody's
time with it. Case dismissed.

680
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:06,843
Thanks, Benjamin. Ahem.

681
00:44:07,011 --> 00:44:09,276
I think I owe you one.

682
00:44:09,447 --> 00:44:12,906
Actually, I think I owe you two.

683
00:44:23,161 --> 00:44:24,185
You knew.

684
00:44:24,362 --> 00:44:25,625
What does she owe you for?

685
00:44:25,796 --> 00:44:28,061
Court will now be in
recess for 20 minutes.

686
00:44:28,232 --> 00:44:30,827
Well, it's, uh...

687
00:44:31,002 --> 00:44:35,906
- It's a long story.
- It's a long walk to the car.

688
00:44:37,375 --> 00:44:41,745
Well, I met her
about five years ago.

689
00:44:41,913 --> 00:44:45,714
She was with, ahem,
Marshall Conley.

690
00:44:45,883 --> 00:44:48,182
- The Supreme Court justice?
- Yeah.

691
00:44:48,352 --> 00:44:51,845
Apparently, they
were having a thing,

692
00:44:52,023 --> 00:44:55,289
and he wanted out, and he
asked me to take her out on a date.

693
00:44:55,459 --> 00:44:57,485
- And did you?
- Yeah.

694
00:44:57,662 --> 00:44:59,563
- Dancing?
- Yeah.

695
00:44:59,730 --> 00:45:02,393
- She like it?
- Yeah.

696
00:45:02,567 --> 00:45:04,866
What'd she say?

697
00:45:05,036 --> 00:45:07,972
She said she liked my suit.

698
00:45:09,473 --> 00:45:12,705
Did you, uh, go
back to her place?

699
00:45:14,579 --> 00:45:16,070
Yeah.

700
00:45:16,480 --> 00:45:19,279
- Music?
- Yeah.

701
00:45:19,450 --> 00:45:21,282
White wine?

702
00:45:21,786 --> 00:45:23,220
Yeah.

703
00:45:23,387 --> 00:45:25,515
Then what?

704
00:45:27,792 --> 00:45:29,317
I ate a hot dog.

705
00:45:29,493 --> 00:45:31,394
Then what?

706
00:45:32,230 --> 00:45:34,563
Well, it was after midnight,

707
00:45:34,732 --> 00:45:39,227
and with all the dancing
and the white wine

708
00:45:39,403 --> 00:45:43,363
and the hot dog with sauerkraut,
I didn't feel good, so I went home.

709
00:45:43,541 --> 00:45:46,272
And that's why she
says she owes you one?

710
00:45:51,115 --> 00:45:52,845
Who knows?

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
